<p>T.L. at your service!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Here&nbsp;lies&nbsp;crit&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Bye&nbsp;bye&nbsp;crit&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>From my understanding, hardly any people/living beings so far have made it to the bottom of the ocean. How was the interdimensional ocean discovered? Is there a reason why the Foundation would need to visit the bottom of the ocean only to coincidentally discover there was another ocean world beneath the lowest point on earth? Adding on, the anomalous properties of the interdimensional ocean and the interdimensional ocean itself doesn’t seem too noticeably anomalous. It just seems like an opening with massive space which ocean water seeped in. With what you’ve given so far, there really isn’t much to differentiate the ocean and the interdimensional one. All the space really is is the ocean but with catatonia inducement and life preserving effects. I’m also unsure of the direction you are taking the narrative. You’ve already stated that the interdimensional ocean causes catatonia and prevents people from getting hurt or dying. There really isn’t much to explore here when you stop to think about it. What makes this interdimensional ocean entirely different and unique from the more known ocean? What exactly is in this interdimensional ocean? What do you want the researchers to discover beyond how it affects people and living beings? How would the researchers be able to research the place and leave without being affected by the properties?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Along the ideas of “if you stare long enough into the abyss…” theming. [&#8230;], it is really more about how the people left alone with what would be consider a “safe” sort of anomaly do their best not to lose themselves in it, and what happens to the things that fail.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why would they consider it something safe if they’re experiencing catatonia? How did they find out they can’t die there? Moreover, I don’t really see this well presented in the concept. How do you plan to execute this in a way that it is present and connects to everything else you have. Also consider if this theme would help benefit your concept or if it may not be an interesting theme to build your concept around. Because from my perspective, there are themes similar to this that have already been explored many times.</p>
<p>Overall, I am not very confident with where this concept is currently at. I think what you should mainly focus on is how to differentiate the two oceans from each other and accentuate the interdimensional ocean’s anomalous properties. I’d also recommend working on the narrative portion for this. Try expanding more on what you want to explore in that world other than the obvious “this is what happens to you here”.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
If you have any questions regarding my critique, please let me know. You are welcome to PM me for another look, or you may follow up with someone from the Butterfly Squad Roster or IRC Chat rooms.
<hr />
<p>Porting over feedback from PM so that other reviewers will have access, and because it's harder for me to keep track of content through PM.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;PM&nbsp;response&nbsp;from&nbsp;author</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;PM&nbsp;response&nbsp;from&nbsp;author</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Hello!</p>
<p>I appreciate your crit, and it has made me realize, like other attempts, I think I have explained my thing poorly, so if you dont mind could i ask you two things about moving forward:</p>
<p>1: The very most basic version of my idea is that i want a deep, underwater location that prioritizes keeping life safe, but is unsettling cause it is attempting to draw all life into it. any advice on how to properly pitch that?</p>
<p>2: If I’m just going to rewrite the pitch, would that warrant creating a new thread? I know the rule against reposting I’m just wondering what would make it a ‘different’ idea enough to post again.</p>
<p>Apologies, and thanks so much!<br /></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;More&nbsp;crit&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Less&nbsp;crit&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The very most basic version of my idea is that i want a deep, underwater location that prioritizes keeping life safe, but is unsettling cause it is attempting to draw all life into it. any advice on how to properly pitch that?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you want to pitch something like that, you are going to need a narrative to show that whole aspect. Think of your template as a theatre stage. Now think of your basic idea as something similar to a script. The only thing you have on stage is the setting (aka the underwater location). That on its own is not enough to carry a whole play. You’ll lose your audience right away. You’re going to need characters for it to interact with which will then build a story. Let the story be influenced by your idea. I think you should try to continue with your last idea for a story to see if it would work out. I don’t really see many narrative options for it other than that.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If I’m just going to rewrite the pitch, would that warrant creating a new thread? I know the rule against reposting I’m just wondering what would make it a ‘different’ idea enough to post again.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You might want to be a little more specific about what part of the pitch you are rewriting (The SCP, anomalous properties, the narrative/story, etc) so that I can better answer your question. If the entire concept is drastically modified to the point where it is considered an entirely different concept from the original version, then yes. However, keep in mind that simply revising a concept does not make it a new concept. Rather, it is a different version of the same concept.<br /></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

