<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The narrative is centered on how the foundation sets up a provisional site at the bottom of the ocean to observe and study this space, for the purpose of research, making sure other deep sea expeditions don't discover it, and to insure the stranger organisms not resembling common ocean life don't accidently drift out and become a problem.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>This seems more like a small paragraph in the description or conprocs, than a narrative.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>When on of the older cremates dies due to deteriorating health both mentally and physically, the others only option is a 'burial at sea'. Of course, as cruel as the Foundation can be, they haven't killed someone and tossed the body into an ocean that preserves life, which ends up happening when the body left out on the rocky sea floor is pulled into the entrance.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Why is this the only option? Why not transport it to the surface and then dispose? This seems incredibly short sighted and stupid of a foundation team specifically going to the bottom of the Ocean to study a magic portal to another ocean, that would preserve life&#8230; to then &quot;bury at sea&quot;</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>leaving it to the next crew to investigate, hinting that this could all happen again.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Like, what is going to &quot;happen again&quot;? Someone is gonna die down there and get buried at sea? that seems unlikely as hell&#8230; ALSO what is the ramifications of this action?</em></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Ultimately, this idea seems rather convoluted to get to a vague ethical dilemma. Why is this portal at the bottom of the ocean but leads to another ocean, that makes you &quot;immortal&quot; sorta? Like it just seems like so many layers to get to &quot;should we have been more careful with this body?&quot;
							</div>

								
					
